---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { navIndex } from '@/lib/posts';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { site, socials } from '@/lib/site';
import { href } from '@/lib/paths';
import { roles, principles } from '@/data/experience';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

const title = 'About';
const description =
  'Samuel Mussie — software engineer in Addis Ababa. Ten years building web and mobile products end to end, across eight languages and three platforms.';

const index = await navIndex('/about');
---

<Layout {title} {description} path="/about" type="profile">
  <!-- ── Header ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 grid-lines opacity-60" aria-hidden="true">
    </div>
    <div class="wrap relative py-16 lg:py-24">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>{index} &nbsp;About</p>
      <div class="mt-7 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <div class="lg:col-span-7">
          <h1
            class="display text-[clamp(2.4rem,6.6vw,4.25rem)]"
            data-reveal
            style="--reveal-delay:60ms"
          >
            Ten years of code.
            <em class="block italic text-accent">Eight languages, three platforms.</em>
          </h1>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-4 lg:col-start-9 lg:pt-3">
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-muted" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:120ms">
            None of that range was a plan. It came from working on other people&rsquo;s stacks,
            which is also where I learned that most projects fail on scoping rather than on code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Narrative ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="wrap py-16 lg:py-24">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-10">
      <div class="lg:col-span-7">
        <div class="prose" data-reveal>
          <p>
            I&rsquo;m Samuel Mussie, a software engineer in Addis Ababa. I build web and mobile
            products end to end — which means I am as comfortable arguing about a foreign key as
            about a keyboard-avoidance animation, and I think being unable to do both is a liability
            rather than a specialism.
          </p>
          <p>
            Currently at <a href={site.companyUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >Mereb Technologies</a
            >, building products for clients in Europe and the US: multi-tenant SaaS platforms,
            procurement and telemedicine systems, mobile apps, and the AI features people now expect
            all of those to have.
          </p>

          <h2>How the range happened</h2>
          <p>
            Before that I spent four years contracting, and contracting does not let you pick your
            stack. A client is already on Java, or PHP, or has an iOS app and no Android one, and
            the job is to be useful inside that — not to explain what you would have chosen.
          </p>
          <p>
            So: Node and Python services, React front ends, a Flutter app, a native Swift app, PHP
            where PHP was already running, and a handful of Telegram bots that solved distinctly
            Ethiopian problems. Eight languages in production is not a badge. It is just what
            happens if you say yes for long enough and refuse to be bad at the unfamiliar half.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it did give me is calibration. When someone asks whether to go native or
            cross-platform, I have shipped both within eighteen months of each other with comparable
            scope, and my answer changes depending on their product rather than on my preference.
          </p>

          <h2>What I am actually good at</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Data modelling that survives the UI.</strong> Screens get redesigned yearly; a badly-named
              column is still there in five years with dashboards depending on it. The schema gets the
              argument, and the front end gets the iteration.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Making failure legible.</strong> Observability, honest error messages, and reproductions
              before fixes. A bug that went away is a bug that is still there.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Shipping thin slices all the way through.</strong> I would rather own a feature
              from migration to deploy than a thick layer of one tier, because that is where you find
              out what your API contract actually cost the client.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>AI features that are mostly plumbing.</strong> Retrieval, chunking, citations, evaluation
              sets, and a hard boundary between the model and anything that writes to a database. I built
              a full RAG system end to end before advising anyone on one, because I refuse to advise from
              blog posts.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Where I still get my hands dirty on my own time</h2>
          <p>
            The side projects are the same instinct as the Flutter-versus-Swift experiment. Fantasy
            Premier League tooling I use every week, a Jeopardy game for team nights, a URL
            shortener built twice to see what the second attempt taught me. If a caching strategy
            survives a Saturday when everyone opens the app at once, it is probably ready for
            someone&rsquo;s production.
          </p>
          <p>
            They are public for two reasons. Small complete programs teach better than large
            incomplete ones. And a commit history going back to 2016 is a more honest CV than
            anything I could write about myself.
          </p>

          <h2>The one I keep coming back to</h2>
          <p>
            In 2020 I built a Telegram bot that <em>tried</em> to translate text into Amharic. I have
            never edited that word out of the repository description, because it was accurate and it dates
            a position I still hold: the AI capability gap for African languages is a data and incentive
            problem, not a model problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Five years and several model generations later, Amharic is still meaningfully behind —
            not because it is harder than Hebrew or Arabic, but because far fewer people have been
            paid to work on it. That is fixable, largely by people who speak the language, which is
            a reasonable argument for where engineering capacity should sit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Sidebar -->
      <aside class="lg:col-span-4 lg:col-start-9">
        <div class="sticky top-24 space-y-8" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:80ms">
          <div class="card rounded-lg p-6">
            <h2 class="font-mono text-2xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-subtle">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="mt-5 space-y-3.5 text-sm">
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Role</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">{site.role}</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Focus</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">Full-stack, web &amp; mobile</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Based in</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">Addis Ababa, ET</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Studied at</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">Addis Ababa University</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Since</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">2016</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Languages</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">Amharic, English</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between gap-4">
                <dt class="text-subtle">Timezone</dt>
                <dd class="text-right text-fg">EAT / GMT+3</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
            <div class="rule my-5"></div>
            <ul class="space-y-2.5">
              {
                socials.map((s) => (
                  <li>
                    <a
                      href={s.href}
                      target={s.href.startsWith('http') ? '_blank' : undefined}
                      rel={s.href.startsWith('http') ? 'noopener noreferrer me' : undefined}
                      class="group flex items-center justify-between gap-3 text-sm text-muted transition-colors hover:text-fg"
                    >
                      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2">
                        <Icon name={s.icon} class="size-3.5 opacity-70" aria-hidden="true" />
                        {s.label}
                      </span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-2xs text-subtle group-hover:text-accent">
                        {s.handle}
                      </span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="card rounded-lg p-6">
            <h2 class="font-mono text-2xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-subtle">
              Beyond the desk
            </h2>
            <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
              <li>
                Fantasy Premier League, seriously enough to have <a
                  href={href('/work/fantasy-pl')}
                  class="link">built tooling for it</a
                >.
              </li>
              <li>
                Reading other people&rsquo;s codebases for fun, which is a strange hobby and the
                most useful one I have.
              </li>
              <li>Advent of Code every December, in Python, badly, happily.</li>
              <li>Arguing that PHP runs more of the world than anyone admits.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Timeline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section
    index="05"
    eyebrow="Timeline"
    title="Where the time went."
    lede="Anything I cannot verify publicly is left general rather than dressed up. If you need exact dates or a formal CV, ask and I will send one."
    id="timeline"
  >
    <ol class="space-y-px overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-line bg-line">
      {
        roles.map((role, i) => (
          <li class="bg-surface p-7 lg:p-9" data-reveal style={`--reveal-delay:${i * 80}ms`}>
            <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
              <div class="lg:col-span-3">
                <p class="flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-2xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-subtle">
                  {role.from && role.to ? `${role.from} — ${role.to}` : 'Earlier'}
                  {role.current && (
                    <span class="relative flex size-1.5">
                      <span class="absolute inline-flex size-full animate-ping rounded-full bg-jade opacity-70" />
                      <span class="relative inline-flex size-1.5 rounded-full bg-jade" />
                    </span>
                  )}
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 text-[0.8125rem] leading-snug text-subtle">{role.location}</p>
              </div>

              <div class="lg:col-span-9">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-medium tracking-tight lg:text-[1.375rem]">{role.title}</h3>
                <p class="mt-1.5 text-[0.9375rem] text-accent">
                  {role.url ? (
                    <a
                      href={role.url}
                      target="_blank"
                      rel="noopener noreferrer"
                      class="link link-accent"
                    >
                      {role.org}
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    role.org
                  )}
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
                  {role.summary}
                </p>
                {role.highlights.length > 0 && (
                  <ul class="mt-5 space-y-2.5">
                    {role.highlights.map((h) => (
                      <li class="relative max-w-2xl pl-5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
                        <span
                          class="absolute left-0 top-[0.72em] h-px w-2.5 bg-accent"
                          aria-hidden="true"
                        />
                        {h}
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                )}
                {role.tags.length > 0 && (
                  <ul class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5" aria-label="Focus areas">
                    {role.tags.map((t) => (
                      <li class="chip">{t}</li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                )}
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── Principles ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <Section
    index="06"
    eyebrow="Positions"
    title="What I will argue about."
    lede="Each of these has cost me a weekend at least once. That is the test."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-12 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        principles.map((p, i) => (
          <div class="border-t border-line pt-6" data-reveal style={`--reveal-delay:${i * 70}ms`}>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
              <Icon name={p.icon} class="size-4 text-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
              <h3 class="text-[1.0625rem] font-medium tracking-tight">{p.title}</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3.5 max-w-lg text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">{p.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>
</Layout>
